  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nSanta Ana winds have weakened since their peak yesterday. Over the \npast 12 hours the top 3 wind gusts were 73 mph at Hellhole Canyon, \n71 mph at Rancho Heights, and 70 mph at Freemont Canyon. Lingering \ngusts in the 35 to 55 mph range can be expected through the \nafternoon mostly in places prone to easterly winds. Winds will \nbecome more confined to wind prone locations into the evening and \novernight hours. Above average temperatures can be expected west \nof the mountains today, with below average temperatures in the \ndeserts. Highs Sunday will run 3 to 7 degrees cooler for the coast\nand 2 to 4 degrees warmer for inland locations. That cooling will\nspread inland through Tuesday. By Tuesday, most locations will \nsee below average temperatures. \n\nModel guidance is in good agreement that a closed low will dig south \nalong the West Coast Monday and Tuesday, before swinging east into \nSouthern California Tuesday night or Wednesday. In addition to \ncooler conditions, this low will bring an increase in westerly winds \nand rain chances. Winds may begin to increase Tuesday afternoon, \npeaking Wednesday or Thursday over the mountains and into the \ndeserts. \n\nRain chances are in the forecast as early Tuesday evening for some \nlocations, but the most likely time for precipitation looks to be \nthis Wednesday and Thursday. Right now, mainly light to moderate \nprecipitation is expected. On Thursday as the low moves directly \nover Southern California it will bring it's associated vorticity \nwith it, increasing the instability of the atmosphere. This \nintroduces a 10 to 15 percent chance of thunderstorms in the coastal \nbasin. If thunderstorms develop, locally heavy rain will occur. Most \nplaces in and west of the mountains have a 50 to 70% chance of \nrainfall totals exceeding 0.50\", with the San Bernardino Mountains \nhaving a higher chance and area deserts a lesser chance. The place \nwith the highest chances of seeing 1\" or more of rain is the San \nBernardino Mountains. Overall, confidence in rain occurring is high \nwith moderate confidence in the details.",
